  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual comparison of how volatile elements and oxygen conditions evolve during magma-ocean stages on a small planet versus a larger, Earth-sized planet. Both halves show layered interiors from atmosphere to magma ocean to metallic core, with blue bubbles representing gas species such as H2, CO, CO2, N2, H2O, SO2, CH4, and H2S.\n\nOn the small-planet side, the system appears relatively open: planetesimal accretion delivers volatiles, primary H2 can be incorporated into the magma ocean and core, and some hydrogen is lost to space. This side emphasizes exchange with incoming planetesimals and a simpler volatile-rich atmosphere.\n\nOn the large-planet side, the diagram highlights redox stratification in the magma ocean, with low oxygen fugacity near the core and higher oxygen fugacity upward, linked to Fe3+/ΣFe. Several models are sketched for how oxidation changes with depth and size, including an fO2 gradient, silicon entering the core, and a perovskite “oxygen pump.” Arrows and reaction labels indicate transfer of Si, O, C, S, and H between core, melt, and crystallizing solids. Overall, the figure conveys that larger planets can develop stronger internal redox gradients and more complex volatile partitioning than smaller bodies.",
